Write a personal essay in which you reflect on moments of insight you have experienced
As I sit now at the bottom of the garden, down among the trees, on the old wooden bench, I reflect on moments of great insight I have experienced throughout my life. One week in particular comes to mind, one which managed to completely redefine my views on life and “progress”. I had been discussing classic literature and Russian philosophy with my maths teacher of all people. He recommended that I read two books without doing any research into their backgrounds, “A Confession” and “The Death of Ivan Illych” by Leo Tolstoy.
“A Confession” had an incredible affect on me and gave me great insight into the complex and wonderful mind of Leo Tolstoy. At the height of his success Tolstoy began to grapple with the meaning of life. He had up until this point brushed aside the topic as he believed that if he chose to simply but his mind to the topic, then the answer would come to him. Upon finally deciding to grapple with the great question of meaning, Tolstoy immediately realises the folly of his ways. At first he played with the idea that life was simply about progress, in the word of the biblical figure King Solomon, "Whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might; for there is no work or device or knowledge or wisdom in the grave where you are going." So Tolstoy did just that, he raised a family, owned a great deal of land, had serfs and servants, and simply “made progress”. During these busy times Tolstoy simply did not have the time to reflect on whether he was ‘doing the right thing’. This aspect of “A Confession” gave me an insight that perhaps the notion of progress is merely a tool we use to shield ourselves from the realisation of our eminent deaths.
Once Tolstoy’s works had become some of the most widely sold and highly renowned books in the world, Tolstoy finally returned to the topic of meaning. He realised that he felt entirely unfulfilled, but why should he? He was on top of the literary world, no wise-man or scholar would deny his company, all the great minds of the world were at his disposal. He had built a wonderful life around himself, he had made “progress”. It was at this moment that Tolstoy came to a heart-shattering conclusion, life was utterly pointless, that man had but three options.  The first option was that he could live an entirely selfish life, never reflecting upon the meaning of life, experiencing more good than bad while his serfs toiled away maintaining his incredibly large estate, an estate whose sole purpose was but to further Tolstoy’s position in the social hierarchy of Russia.  The second option is the one that Tolstoy believed to be the easiest and most honourable, if you don’t like a room then leave, a man could simply end his own life. This was the option that Tolstoy longed for but he felt himself to weak to carry it out, so then comes the third option. The third option that Tolstoy outlines is to simply keep living, despite the meaninglessness of the entire endeavour, despite the pain and suffering. In order to stop himself from committing suicide Tolstoy removed all the ropes from his room and he stopped hunting with a gun. This was the part of “A Confession” that gave me an insight into myself that I was unaware of up until that point. While I have always been nihilistic, I had not realised that I had been living through the viewpoint of the third option my entire life, simply existing day-to-day. At the time I found it difficult to believe that despite Tolstoy’s incredible success, he was entirely unhappy with the literary empire he had constructed, that he despised the privileges that he had been granted from birth as a member of the nobility.
The insights that “A Confession” granted me have, even to this day, an incredible effect on my world-view. Is it selfish that I wish to experience more the positive things in life than the negative? Is it selfish that I wish to one day have a family, with children who will also have to face the bleak realities of a world a believe to be entirely without meaning or reason? Does it even matter? All of these are questions which entire my mind daily. It is my belief that once you think about the cruel reality of meaning, that nothing is for certain, you will simply never be able to rid yourself of these those thoughts. These reflections as I sit on my bench have given me further insight into my “person”, me the small speck, on small green planet, in a big and seemingly uncaring universe.
“The Death of Ivan Ilyc­h” is a fantastic, yet unintentional companion-piece to “A Confession”. It is easy to see Ivan Illych as Leo Tolstoy’s representation for his own life. Ivan Illych ­is an everyday, career man. Overtime he has taken on the charateristics of someone of high social standing. He is materialstic and cares only for what is “proper”. Ivan marries but when his wife becomes pregnant Ivan begins to distance himself from her. He believes her “behaviour” is inconveniant for his work which he values above all else. At work he prides himself in being able to distance himself from all personal concerns. Ivan finds a new job and purchases a new home which he plans to decorate before his family arrives. While hanging curtains Ilych falls from a step-ladder and hits his side on the windowsill. The injury does not seem to be serious and overall Ilych is quite pleased with the appearance of his new dwelling. Sometime later Ivan begins to experience pain in his side and a peculiar taste in is mouth. Ivan pays for the finest doctors yet they all disagree on the nature of his ailment. Ivan realises that his death is iminent yet he does not even obtain sympathy from his wife who struggles to hide her disdain for her emotionally vacant husband. Ivan is struck by the gravity of his own mortality in the final weeks of his life. As he lies in his deathbed he is comforted only by the shallowist form of company, buisness partners whishing for the signing of documents. Ivan is alone, left to grapple with the cold grasp of death. Ivan wonders if he had “lived his life correctly”, had he just wasted it all in the persuit of the commendments of his peers. During his final moments Ivan manages a final act, he places his hand upon his son’s head, just as his wife approaches his bed with a tear stricken face. Ivan realises at this moment that his official life, family and social relations were all artifiicial. Upon this moment of realisation, Ivan Ilych is struck by an emense sense of joy, he stretches out and dies. Those who surround him in his final moments do not weep, secretly they are relieved, relieved it is not them who has died, wondering what they have inherited and what promotions now awaited them. This heartbreaking story by Leo Tolstoy gave me great insight into the human condition. Despite all of the “progress” we make in life, the relations we forge, the money we make, in the end we all must die. Ivan Ilych, just as Tolstoy, held the perceptions of others and the persuit of progress above anything else, and in the end was left feeling empty and defeated.
